  "text": "We are asked to compute the value of the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix} \\sin a & \\sin b & \\sin c \\\\ \\sin b & \\sin c & \\sin a \\\\ \\sin c & \\sin a & \\sin b \\end{vmatrix} + \n\\begin{vmatrix} \\cos a & \\cos b & \\cos c \\\\ \\cos b & \\cos c & \\cos a \\\\ \\cos c & \\cos a & \\cos b \\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the structure of the determinants\n\nBoth determinants are **3x3 matrices** with a **cyclic pattern** of sine or cosine terms. Let's denote the first determinant as $ D_1 $ and the second as $ D_2 $.\n\n- $ D_1 = \\begin{vmatrix} \\sin a & \\sin b & \\sin c \\\\ \\sin b & \\sin c & \\sin a \\\\ \\sin c & \\sin a & \\sin b \\end{vmatrix} $\n- $ D_2 = \\begin{vmatrix} \\cos a & \\cos b & \\cos c \\\\ \\cos b & \\cos c & \\cos a \\\\ \\cos c & \\cos a & \\cos b \\end{vmatrix} $\n\nEach matrix is a **cyclic permutation** of the three variables $ a, b, c $, and the entries are either $ \\sin $ or $ \\cos $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use symmetry and properties of determinants\n\nLet’s consider the structure of the first determinant:\n\n$$\nD_1 = \n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\sin a & \\sin b & \\sin c \\\\ \n\\sin b & \\sin c & \\sin a \\\\ \n\\sin c & \\sin a & \\sin b \n\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis matrix is a **cyclic permutation** of the rows (or columns). In general, if a matrix is a **cyclic permutation** of its rows or columns, the determinant is **zero** **if the rows or columns are linearly dependent**.\n\nBut in this case, the rows are **not** linearly dependent, because they are **distinct** vectors. However, the key insight is that this matrix is **a circulant matrix** with entries $ \\sin a, \\sin b, \\sin c $, and the rows are cyclic permutations of this.\n\nBut here’s the key: the **determinant of such a circulant matrix is zero** **if the entries are not all the same**.\n\nBut wait — more importantly, we can **compute the determinant** of this matrix directly.\n\nLet’s denote the rows as:\n\n- Row 1: $ R_1 = [\\sin a, \\sin b, \\sin c] $\n- Row 2: $ R_2 = [\\sin b, \\sin c, \\sin a] $\n- Row 3: $ R_3 = [\\sin c, \\sin a, \\sin b] $\n\nNow, observe that:\n\n- $ R_2 = R_1 $ **shifted** by 1 position (cyclic shift)\n- $ R_3 = R_1 $ **shifted** by 2 positions\n\nThis is a **cyclic permutation** of the rows. The **determinant of a matrix whose rows are cyclic permutations of a vector is zero** **if the vector is not a constant vector**.\n\nBut in our case, the entries are not all the same, so the rows are not linearly independent. Hence, the **determinant is zero**.\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nD_1 = 0\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the cosine matrix:\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\begin{vmatrix} \\cos a & \\cos b & \\cos c \\\\ \\cos b & \\cos c & \\cos a \\\\ \\cos c & \\cos a & \\cos b \\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is the same structure as $ D_1 $, but with **cosines** instead of sines. Again, the rows are cyclic permutations of each other. So the determinant is also **zero**.\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nD_2 = 0\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add the two determinants\n\n$$\nD_1 + D_2 = 0 + 0 = 0\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{0}\n$$",
